## 2. Professional code quality (big-company / enterprise bar)

### MUST

- Write code as if it will be reviewed by a senior eng team at a serious company shipping a flagship app: clear names, explicit types where the stack expects them, bounded error handling, no silent `catch`, no dead stubs left in user paths.
- Prefer **root-cause fixes** over bandaids (timers to hide races, “refresh everything” to mask cache bugs, stripping libraries to avoid fixing them).
- Keep changes **scoped and coherent**: touch what the task needs; leave unrelated refactors for a separate pass unless they block correctness.
- Preserve proven interaction systems (drag/drop, selection, virtualization, IPC contracts). Extending is fine; silent rewrites are not.
- Match repository conventions (file layout, naming, component patterns, lint rules). Do not introduce a parallel architecture for one feature.
- Structure for maintainability: small focused modules, obvious data flow, testable boundaries where the repo already tests.
- Comments only for non-obvious intent, invariants, or protect-notes — never narrate what the code already says.
- Accessibility and keyboard basics on interactive surfaces the user will use (focus, labels, Esc/Enter where expected) — professional apps do not ship mouse-only traps by accident.

### MUST NOT

- Leave `TODO`, placeholder UI, “Coming soon,” or fake data in paths the user will click in this task’s scope.
- Paper over bugs by deleting capability (e.g. removing Three.js because 3D preview was hard).
- Duplicate near-identical components/services instead of extending the real one.
- Claim “production ready” while knowing build, type, or runtime failures remain.
- Commit secrets, machine-specific paths, or debug dumps into product code.

---

## 3. Research mandate — GitHub + web for references, assets, resources, and code

Before and during non-trivial work, **look outward**. Do not invent from memory when the web has better ground truth.

### MUST research when

- **Planning** a feature or redesign → how top products and strong open-source projects solve it.
- **Designing** a surface → craft references + asset sources (see §1 / §1.1).
- **Implementing** a non-trivial subsystem → proven libraries, official docs, exemplar GitHub repos, migration guides.
- **Debugging** → exact error text, GitHub Issues/PRs, release notes, known breaking changes.
- **Choosing packages** → compare maintenance, license, API fit, and **current** major/stable versions on the registry.

### What to pull from GitHub and the web

| Need | Where / how |
|------|-------------|
| UX / visual references | Competing products, design galleries, project visual refs (e.g. Uiverse) |
| Implementation patterns | Official docs, high-quality GitHub repos, READMEs, example apps |
| **Code to adapt** | Permissively licensed snippets/modules from maintained repos — **adapt** into project conventions; do not vendor-dump trees |
| Assets | SVG/PNG packs, GitHub `raw` assets, generator tools (§1.1) |
| Resources | RFCs, API references, package docs, changelogs, design tokens, architecture notes |
| Packages | npm, NuGet, crates.io, etc. — verify versions exist before wiring |
| Breakages | GitHub Issues/PRs, changelogs, migration guides |

### MUST NOT

- Copy large copyrighted or proprietary code/assets verbatim.
- Dump vendor trees into the repo and call it “integration done.”
- Cite imaginary APIs, stars, or package versions — verify with docs or registry first.
- Ignore license terms when adapting third-party code or assets.

```text
❌ BAD: hand-roll a fragile thumbnail cache because “we can write it quick”
✅ GOOD: research maintained libs + exemplar repos → adopt / deepen the META path already in the project
```

