## 4. META methods — latest proven approach for implementation **and** planning

“META” means: **use the best current, proven approach** — not the first hack that compiles, and not a plan that ignores how serious products actually build the thing.

### MUST (implementation)

- Prefer **battle-tested libraries and packages** over thin hand-rolled reimplementations when the domain is non-trivial (shell/FS, metadata, archives, imaging, search, IPC, UI, parsers, caches, graphics).
- Deepen existing services and product surfaces (list columns, preview, search, settings, plugins) instead of wrapping basics in a forbidden admin iframe / sidecar / HTTP bridge when project rules ban that.
- Pick approaches that scale when the feature is user-visible and performance-sensitive: pooling, cancellation, virtualization, incremental indexing, GPU/compositor-friendly rendering, streaming.
- When multiple implementation paths exist, choose the one that is (1) correct, (2) maintainable, (3) aligned with repo architecture — then implement that one **fully**.
- Stay current: prefer **modern stable APIs** and maintained majors; read breaking-change notes before upgrades; do not copy year-old blog code when the library has a newer recommended API.

### MUST (planning)

- Before proposing or executing a non-trivial plan, spend a research beat on **how the best systems do this now** (docs + GitHub + package ecosystems).
- Plans must name the **META stack** (libraries/packages/APIs), the **integration surface** (where it shows up for the user), and the **proof** (builds/checks) — not only UI adjectives.
- Reject plans that are teardown-only, cosmetic-only, or “skin a stub and call it META.”

### 4.1 Stack & packages — component libraries, npm, .NET, and more

Treat the package ecosystem as a **first-class tool**, not an afterthought.

| Layer | Expectation |
|-------|-------------|
| **UI component libraries / primitives** | Prefer mature headless or styled libraries already aligned with the repo, or newly justified ones (accessible primitives, virtualizers, flow/canvas, charts, etc.). Adapt to product tokens — do not drop in a second unrelated design system skin. |
| **npm / JS / TS** | Use well-maintained packages for real problems (state, virtualization, parsers, media, 3D, etc.). Check the registry for current stable versions. |
| **NuGet / .NET** | Prefer proven packages in the project’s ecosystem (shell, metadata, imaging, archives, IPC helpers, etc.). Bump carefully; read release notes. |
| **Other ecosystems** | Same bar for any stack the repo uses (crates, pip, etc.): maintained, license-clear, justified. |
| **Platform APIs** | Prefer modern first-party platform APIs when they are the META path (Win32/WinRT, WebView2, etc.) over brittle reimplementation. |

### MUST NOT

- Call something “META” when it is only a cosmetic skin over a stub.
- Add a dependency for vanity; every package must earn its keep.
- Perform drive-by major version upgrades across the whole tree unless the task explicitly scopes that.
- Fight the repo by introducing a parallel component library that conflicts with the existing visual language without an explicit decision.

